  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionCode": "book6-article-013-body",
      "heading": "Reading the Book VI structure",
      "anchor": "article-body",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Decolonization transferred sovereignty to new states while often leaving administrative borders, infrastructure and institutional interfaces created under colonial rule. India's post-independence choices show how a new state can inherit structures while building its own combination of political, economic and civilizational continuity."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Inheritance is not treated as simple continuation, and independence is not treated as a complete restart. Reconfiguration occurs through combinations of retention, rejection, modification and new construction."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "This article distinguishes historical fact, historical reconstruction and structural interpretation in the manuscript. Later outcomes are not treated as inevitable at the time, and motives or causal claims not present in the manuscript are not added."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
